  "beats": [
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:01Z",
      "text": "The Day of the Dead is traditionally celebrated on November 1 and 2, though other days, such as October 31 or November 6, may be included depending on the locality.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:08Z",
      "text": "At midnight on November 2, people light candles and ride winged boats called mariposas to Janitzio, a lake island with a cemetery, to honor and celebrate the dead there.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:117"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:15Z",
      "text": "In Italy, November 2 is All Souls' Day, colloquially known as Day of the Dead or Giorno dei Morti.",
      "cites": [
        "wiki:rev:1380361744:160"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:33Z",
      "text": "On 2 November 2026, the English article drew 63,975 readers, about 13× the usual daily figure.",
      "cites": [
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:2026-11-02",
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:prior"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:40Z",
      "text": "The article appeared in 4 languages, and after English, the Spanish version drew the most readers, with 19,498.",
      "cites": [
        "views:en:Day_of_the_Dead:2026-11-02",
        "views:es:Día_de_Muertos:2026-11-02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:47Z",
      "text": "On the globe, each language glows where it is mainly spoken, brighter for more readers, and a language is not the same as a country.",
      "cites": [
        "views:es:Día_de_Muertos:2026-11-02",
        "views:de:Tag_der_Toten:2026-11-02",
        "views:ja:死者の日_(メキシコ):2026-11-02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "2026-11-02T00:00:57Z",
